            This is in contrast to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+composition"} vertical composition
            of 2-morphisms, which is their composition along 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
            s.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Horizontal and vertical composition are subject to the compatibility condition called the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchange+law"} interchange law
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            then the composition of 1-cells between them defines a functor.
